{
  "route": "/food/badi-chura/",
  "save_id": "food:badi-chura",
  "title": "Badi chura",
  "lead": "Look beyond the dish name: follow a preparation, notice its texture, and find its place in a meal.",
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "opening",
      "heading": null,
      "text": "A small accompaniment can leave a large question unanswered: how did it become the food in front of us? With badi chura, it helps to follow the work backwards from the serving bowl. Ask what was done to the badi before it was crushed, then what joined it."
    },
    {
      "id": "preparation",
      "heading": "From dried badi to chura",
      "text": "Sasmita describes badi as sun-dried lentil dumplings, made from black gram in her account. Her preparation fries or dry-roasts them, then pulses them with onion, garlic and green chilli into a coarse mixture. Mustard oil goes into the pan for frying, or into the mixture when the badi is dry-roasted."
    },
    {
      "id": "reading-method",
      "heading": null,
      "text": "That choice gives us something specific to ask a cook about. Instead of treating an ingredient list as the whole recipe, follow the order of the work. Which step would they change, and what would they want that change to achieve? Their answer belongs to their own preparation; it need not settle how everyone else should cook."
    },
    {
      "id": "meal",
      "heading": "Beside the rice",
      "text": "In her 2024 Odisha Tourism account, Pritisha Das places badi chura beside pakhala, among several accompaniments. She also describes a preference for pickle on her platter. It is a named contributor’s meal account, not a compulsory menu."
    },
    {
      "id": "closing",
      "heading": null,
      "text": "These accounts offer two ways to begin a conversation: follow the making, or ask about the meal. A useful contribution could connect them through one person’s version—who made it, what they chose to do, and what they ate alongside it. Keep the maker’s name and explanation with the story, and ask permission before sharing their words."
    }
  ],
  "sources": [
